foreign  I got this direct message I'll just say  it's from Jack Jack if you're listening  you know who you are  um I'm super glad you reached out here's  what Jack wrote  I love your show John  I'm a redneck tough Midwest guy and I  enjoy the heck out of it dude I already  like you jack we're already we're  already good friends  one thing I gotta ask  why in every episode you tell someone to  pick up a rock and drop it I tried it  and the whole time I think about how  stupid it feels instead of getting rid  of the grief am I doing it wrong  dude great question Jack thanks for  sending that on the internet  um for sliding into my DMs I think this  is how the kit nope that's not nope  that's not what they said okay  um thank you for sending me an  electronic message from the Midwest Jack  um okay a couple things  carrying around throwing a rock picking  up and throwing it is not going to heal  your grief okay  when I tell somebody to go to the store  and buy a brick buy a cinder block and  write on it what's this thing you're  carrying around  whether that's hatred for somebody rage  for somebody an inability to forgive  yourself  I want you to carry it  past the point where it's comfortable  obviously don't get injured don't do  anything it's gonna  cause you harm  but I want your body to feel how heavy  this is  I want you to carry it around to the  part that your shoulders are heavy your  hands are heavy it's it's scraping on  your hand it's just heavy  and then I want you to do walk all the  way you can to the back corner of your  backyard for some of you living in  condos or apartments that's not very far  walk you may go out to a park for some  of y'all in a big place it might be a  long walk  but the whole thing is a miniature  pilgrimage but from  where I am right now to I am going down  and I'm going to set this thing down and  when you set it down  and you stand up  that lightness that freedom feeling  I want your body to get a tiny little  glimpse of what it's going to feel like  when you're not carrying around somebody  else's anger when you're not carrying  around somebody else's bad decisions  that they made that you still hate that  drunk driver that took your dad 14 years  ago or seven years ago  that anger in that rage is not helping  your dad come back it's not further  punishing the drunk driver it's just  making your life  more miserable  and so Jack dude is just a metaphor for  how heavy these things are that we carry  we don't realize  that the poison that we carry has a  physiological consequence every time we  spin up and think up about that drunk  driver our body starts the threat  response system again and let's dump  cortisol and adrenaline into the  bloodstream and get this sucker fired up  again because we gotta fight or we gotta  run from something and there's no way to  fight anymore there's nobody to run from  you become Your Own Worst Enemy  and so Jack uh it's just simply a  metaphor walking out your backyard and  just picking up a rock and being like  I looked at a bad website and then  throwing it on the ground that's not the  point the point is to say I'm carrying  this thing around and I want to stop and  it also is not going to assist with your  grief at all in fact it will amplify  your grief  it will free you up to finally feel  I'm trying to think sometimes whenever I  go rocking I've got my goruck bag and  I've got it filled with some weights and  I would go walk it's so heavy and all I  can feel are the straps in my back all I  can feel is my shoulders tense and me  hunched oh it's just so heavy  I don't feel how much my feet hurt and  how much my knees hurt  until I take it off I drop that thing  and then I realized oh that's how tired  I am oh that's my knees or girl like I  need to stretch whatever the thing is  then I can finally feel my entire body  but until I take that backpack off I'm  just feeling where those straps are  digging in  and so we often carry around anger or  rage and we think it's just them it's  just them it's just that it's us we're  the ones paying the price for that  hatred that rage that frustration that  lack of forgiveness it's us  and so really it's just an exercise it's  a metaphor  um the feedback I've received has just  been second to none  folks who said okay I'm gonna do this  and I'm gonna set it down and when it  pops back in your mind the next day you  can be like nope I put that down in the  backyard I've got a place where I put it  down it's like going to a funeral I've  got a point when I said goodbye to my  grandmother I got a point when I said  goodbye to my kids  we got a funeral and we said goodbye  it's it's that process so  um thanks for your question man I love  you dude and I hope you come visit us  here in Nashville sometime I'd love to  um I was gonna say buy you a cup of  coffee but it's free here in the lobby  if y'all ever want to come visit but  um thank you so so much for that  question Jack everybody  you got something in your past It's  haunting you that you've done that was  done to you try it  go buy a cinder block pay the money go  through the process buy it put a piece  of duct tape on it or a piece of masking  tape on it write down what happened  just carry it around with you  15 minutes 20 minutes some of you may  only be able to carry it for two minutes  right  and then take it somewhere where it's  going to sit down forever maybe you go  on a hike with it out in a in a park and  you carry it out into the woods and you  set it down and say this is where this  this is where this gets left forever  and then every time you think of it in  the future you realize it's already over  there give that a shot let me know how  it goes

